# The rename pass against the stress corpus: 558 targets of macro-heavy,
# template-heavy C++. googletest is the corpus the rename work was developed
# against; abseil is the independent one, and it is what finished the job.
#
# Measured: 5281 edits across 275 files apply with 0 conflicts, //absl/...
# rebuilds clean, and a second pass has nothing left to do. Getting here from
# 753 errors in 12 files took, in order: an OffsetOfExpr visitor (offsetof on a
# member was invisible, in plain non-template code); the recorder no longer
# taking the implicit unnamed-field node of an anonymous union for "a member not
# being renamed" (StatusOr's status_, and raw_hash_set's ctrl_); a per-use check
# that the new name still binds from the class the use goes through (StatusOr's
# method status() would have hidden the renamed base field); a raw-lexer audit
# that declines any spelling of an old name no rewrite-reachable reference
# accounts for; declining the *name* of a dependent token no instantiation ever
# resolves (kIsNothrow inside an alias template's argument, folded to a value);
# declining members referenced from files nobody rewrites (a textual_hdrs .inc);
# and the aspect passing each target's own copts (randen_hwaes.cc is behind
# ABSL_HAVE_ACCELERATED_AES, which only the target's -maes turns on).
#
# What is left is declined and reported, not broken: about 190 declarations,
# most by the spelling audit and the unmapped-instantiation backstop. Those are
# the recall work, not correctness work.
#
# NB: this file is sourced by bash, so values must not contain backticks or the
# dollar sign.
REPO=abseil-cpp
RULESET=member_snake_case
EXPECT=pass
